// Jest Snapshot v1, https://jestjs.io/docs/snapshot-testing

exports[`CLI help interface for 'idm remote-proxy setup' should be expected english 1`] = `
"Usage: frodo idm remote-proxy setup [options] [host] [realm] [username] [password]

[Cloud-only] Set up an IDM remote proxy from this tenant to another one: the
whole workflow in one command, between two saved connection profiles.

On the receiving tenant it creates an OAuth2 client with a generated secret and
the static user mapping that lets the client act as the IDM administrator, which
is admin-equivalent access. On this tenant it creates an ESV secret that holds
the secret, an ESV variable that holds the host name of the receiving tenant,
and the remote proxy, which refers to both by placeholder so that it can be
promoted to another environment. It then applies the ESV changes, which restarts
services and takes about three minutes (up to ten), and tests the proxy until it
answers. The generated secret is never shown or logged.

It is safe to run again: it completes what is missing and leaves what exists as
it is, except with --rotate-secret. It asks for confirmation before it starts.

Arguments:
  host                       AM base URL, e.g.: https://cdk.iam.example.com/am.
                             To use a connection profile, just specify a unique
                             substring or alias.
  realm                      Realm. Specify realm as '/' for the root realm or
                             'realm' or '/parent/child' otherwise. (default:
                             "alpha" for Identity Cloud tenants, "/" otherwise.)
  username                   Username to login with. Must be an admin user with
                             appropriate rights to manage authentication
                             journeys/trees. If given without a password, and it
                             matches the username already stored in the
                             connection profile for the target host, frodo uses
                             that profile's stored password instead of requiring
                             it on the command line.
  password                   Password.

Deployment: Cloud-only

Options:
  --allow-failures           Report operations that fail, but still exit with
                             code 0. Without this option the command runs every
                             operation, prints a summary of what failed and
                             exits with code 1.
  --client-id <client-id>    OAuth2 client on the receiving tenant. Default:
                             frodo-remote-proxy-<name of this tenant>.
  --dry-run                  Show what would be created on which tenant and
                             change nothing.
  -i, --proxy-id <proxy-id>  Name of the remote proxy. Default: the name of the
                             receiving tenant.
  --no-apply                 Do not apply the ESV changes. Apply them with frodo
                             esv apply, then check the proxy with frodo idm
                             remote-proxy test.
  --no-wait                  Don't wait for the ESV changes to finish applying
                             and for the proxy to answer.
  --rotate-secret            Write a new secret to the OAuth2 client and the ESV
                             secret even if both exist. The proxy fails until
                             the ESV changes are applied.
  --target <host url>        Host URL of the receiving tenant. The URL must
                             resolve to an existing connection profile. (pick
                             from connection profile) (prompts if missing)
  --target-realm <realm>     Realm of the OAuth2 client on the receiving tenant.
                             (default: alpha)
  --timeout <seconds>        How long to wait for the ESV changes to apply and
                             for the proxy to answer. Only effective without
                             --no-wait. (default: 600 secs (10 mins))
  -y, --yes                  Do not ask for confirmation.
  -h, --help                 Help
  -hh, --help-more           Help with all options.
  -hhh, --help-all           Help with all options, environment variables, and
                             usage examples.
"
`;
